41 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 24 0 R
/E (The problem. IoT devices \(sensors, cameras, GPS trackers, smart locks\) talk to servers over networks anyone can listen to. A server that only trusts a device ID, or a password sent over the network, can be fooled by spoofing, by a replayed recording of a valid login, or by one leaked shared secret that exposes every device. Attackers also push malicious firmware that devices install blindly, and the one master key that protects every device secret is a single file that can be stolen or lost.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
42 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 24 0 R
/E (Why it matters. Unattended, always-on devices are easy doors into homes, factories and vehicle fleets. One forged identity or poisoned update can compromise the whole network.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
43 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 24 0 R
/E (The need. Prove a device owns a secret without ever sending the secret, prove an update really comes from its maker, and make sure no single person or file holds the master key.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
